Option	Short Description
Continue	❗ Ignores the error and keeps running the next sampler in the thread.
👉 Use this if errors are expected and shouldn't stop the test.
Start Next Thread Loop	🚫 Skips the rest of the current iteration, and starts a new loop iteration for the same thread.
👉 Useful if each loop represents a business flow and you want to abandon the current one on error.
Stop Thread	🔚 Stops only this one user/thread from running further. Others continue.
👉 Use this when one user's flow fails critically and should not proceed.
Stop Test	⛔ Completely stops the entire test, but finishes the current samplers already in execution.
👉 Good for critical failures that affect the whole system.
Stop Test Now	🚨 Immediately kills the test and terminates all running threads without waiting.
👉 Use with caution; good for emergencies like server crashes or data corruption.



🔹 1. Thread Behavior (Execution Flow)
JMeter will start 50 users.
It will start them over a 5-second ramp-up period → so 10 users/sec.
Each user will start executing your samplers (steps 01 to 08) in a loop (Loop Count = Infinite).
But since you specified a 30-second duration, each thread/user will keep looping through the test until 30 seconds have passed.
Once 30 seconds is up, no new loops start, and the test will shut down gracefully.
🔹 2. On Success:
Each user goes through the full sequence (01 to 08).
After finishing, they loop again if time is left (within 30 sec).
This continues until time expires.
🔹 3. On Failure (Sampler Error):
Because Continue is selected:
If any sampler (like login, search, etc.) fails (e.g., 500 error, timeout), JMeter will ignore the error and move to the next sampler.
The thread keeps executing until 30 seconds are over — errors won’t stop the test.
⚠️ Common Misunderstanding
You mentioned:
"I thought it's going to be running the whole thread group within 30 seconds."
🛑 It doesn’t mean "each user runs once within 30 seconds."
✅ It means: JMeter will keep all 50 users running in loops for 30 seconds.
So:
Some users may loop 2 or 3 times.
Others may not finish a second loop depending on how long the steps take.


Most Likely Cause: Missing Cookie/Session Handling
By default, JMeter doesn't share cookies across samplers unless you explicitly add:
🧩 Cookie Manager:
Test Plan > Thread Group > Add > Config Element > HTTP Cookie Manager
Stores and reuses cookies automatically across requests (like a browser).
Is required for most session-based web apps.


🔧 How to Fix This — Step-by-Step
✅ Add HTTP Cookie Manager
Right-click Thread Group → Add → Config Element → HTTP Cookie Manager
No extra config needed (leave defaults).
(Optional) Add Cache Manager
Right-click Thread Group → Add → Config Element → HTTP Cache Manager
Simulates browser cache — not always needed but helpful in some flows.
🔍 Re-run your test
Watch the login step and see if the 500 goes away.
Also check if session/cookies are being passed (View Results Tree → Request tab).














